AD  - School of Engineering and Applied Science, Hofstra University, Hempstead, NY, United States
AB  - This research-to-practice full paper examines students' perceptions of human dispositions and their role in facilitating knowledge acquisition and skill development. Unfortunately, many universities teach computing or engineering from a knowledge-based perspective rather than a competency-based approach that requires three components-knowledge, skills, and human dispositions-as outlined in the Computing Curricula 2020 report. Graduates of knowledge-based programs may not have the competence to be productive in the workplace. To study the effects of human disposition, 109 undergraduate students enrolled in the engineering and information systems management program evaluated the importance of seven dispositions: inventive, meticulous, self-directed, purpose-driven, proactive, collaborative, and adaptable. Students ranked these dispositions on a scale ranging from essential to insignificant and reflected on their engagement during formative assignments. The findings showed that students considered all seven dispositions highly relevant to their learning experience. Moreover, fostering an understanding of individual dispositions contributes to the development of competency, enabling students to approach workplace challenges with greater confidence and effectiveness. This work provides educators with insights into the role of human dispositions in curriculum design, course development, and syllabus creation. It emphasizes the importance of aligning educational practices with the attributes essential for computing and engineering professionals. © 2025 IEEE.
