AB  - Formation of a curriculum is a process, which requires considering a huge number of constraints, conditions, parameters, and therefore even partial automation and unification of this process can significantly facilitate the work of curriculum architects. Our studies are aimed at both complexity reduction of the curriculum formation in higher computing education and improving the quality of certain educational programs or curriculum. The formal problem statement is defined for two purposes: correction of an existing curriculum and the formation of a new one. The proposed algorithm for the problem solution is based on the competence weight matrix, obtained as a result of learning outcomes analysis. The analysis is implemented with mathematical statistics methods such as the Rasch measurement model, the Bloom’s taxonomy using a specifically developed framework. The proposed framework is applied for analysis and comparison of different learning strategies and learning progress. The case study uses a data set, which contains the data about studying more than 600 students over 5 years. The curriculum analysis and correction are shown for the system software subjects included in computer science bachelor degree programs. Also, some terminological aspects of the competence model and educational trajectories are discussed. We consider modern learning management system capabilities of providing necessary statistics, used as source data for the analysis outcomes. As a result of the research, we formulate a holistic competence-based approach to curriculum formation in computing education. This article may be useful for curriculum developers and university professors. © 2021, The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG.
