AD  - Computer Science & ITS, El Paso Community College, El Paso, TX, United States
AB  - Cybersecurity threats are growing in sophistication, exposing the limitations of preparing computing professionals through technical training alone. While adversarial thinking - anticipating malicious actions, challenging assumptions, and viewing systems from an attacker's perspective, is widely recognized as a core element of cybersecurity education, it is too often treated as a discrete technical skill. This narrow framing overlooks its potential as a professional disposition: an enduring, internalized mindset that shapes how individuals approach problems, assess risks, and make ethical decisions across computing domains. This paper reframes adversarial thinking as a disposition within a competency-based education framework, which integrates knowledge, skills, and dispositions to define the expectations for computing professionals. Drawing from the ACM/IEEE-CS/AAAI CS2023 Curricula guidelines and the NSA/DHS CAE-CD knowledge units, the authors analyze points of intersection that support embedding adversarial thinking into curriculum design. Our study focuses on a two-year college designated as a National Center of Academic Excellence, leveraging strong partnerships with industry and four-year institutions. Input from industry advisory board members is used to identify workforce-valued characteristics of an adversarial mindset, complementing ethical reasoning, critical analysis, and resilience. By articulating adversarial thinking as a cross-domain competency, we provide curricular strategies and assessment approaches - such as reflective portfolios and project-based learning - that enable students to demonstrate these dispositions in authentic contexts. This work contributes to the ongoing discourse on professional dispositions in computing education, offering a model for integrating adversarial thinking into IT and cybersecurity programs to strengthen workforce preparedness and adaptability in the face of evolving threats.  © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
