AB  - Kenya underwent a curriculum reform from 8-4-4 to a Competency-Based Curriculum(CBC), introducing coding with Scratch at the primary level of education, which was new to the majority of the in-service teachers, leaving them ill-equipped to teach it. This necessitated the equipping of the teachers with skills and confidence to implement coding with Scratch in the Schools. Besides efforts by the government and other stakeholders, there still remains a huge skills gap, especially in rural areas. Therefore, this study presents the design and delivery of a 12-week blended Teacher Professional Development(TPD) program on introduction to coding with Scratch for in-service primary school teachers implemented in rural areas of Western Kenya. This TPD combined in-person workshops, self-paced online learning, monthly webinars and a Community of Practice(CoP) facilitated on WhatsApp. The main aim of this study was to assess the change in confidence levels and content knowledge of teachers in using Scratch before and after the training. It also examined how the contextualization of the coding course delivered in a blended approach, subjective norms, perceived usefulness and their interests impact their readiness to integrate coding in the classroom. Using a mixed methods approach, data was collected from 28 volunteer participants of the study by administering pre- and post-surveys and conducting semi-structured interviews. The results showed that content knowledge and confidence of teachers in using Scratch improved after the training. Additionally, the blended delivery approach, contextualization of the course materials and subjective norms highly correlated with teachers' readiness to integrate coding with Scratch in the classroom. Interest and confidence showed a marginal effect on readiness, while perceived usefulness had the weakest non-significant effect on readiness. These findings highlight the importance of providing teachers with locally contextualized content, delivered through a blended approach supported by the education stakeholders to allow effective upskilling of teachers. These are useful results for education policy makers to enable the development of policies that support Blended TPDs.  © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
