TI  - Improving Self-Efficacy Through Experiential Learning of IoT Programming
PY  - 2025
T2  - TALE 2025 - 2025 IEEE International Conference on Teaching, Assessment, and Learning for Engineering, Proceedings
DO  - 10.1109/TALE66047.2025.11346629
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105033217199?origin=resultslist
AD  - Toyo University, Faculty of Information Networking for Innovation and Design (INIAD), Tokyo, Japan
AB  - The pervasive adoption of Internet of Things (IoT) technology necessitates reforms in digital education, particularly in competency-based approaches that emphasize real-world problem-solving. As reported by the Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD), while Japanese students demonstrate high academic achievement, their self-efficacy regarding computers remains notably low despite comparable interest levels. This paper addresses this disparity by developing and evaluating IoT programming educational materials designed to enhance self-efficacy through practical, culturally relevant experiences. Drawing upon Kolb's Experiential Learning theory and Culturally Responsive Teaching (CRT) approaches, the materials leverage the familiar and urgent social problem of summer heat stroke in Japan. The materials utilize temperature and humidity sensors to calculate WBGT, a widely used index in Japan for alerting about heat stroke risks. Utilizing micro:bit as the educational platform, the developed education materials enable systematic learning of basic programming processes and data transmission through the four phases of Kolb's theory: concrete experience, reflective observation, abstract conceptualization, and active experimentation. The educational effectiveness of these materials was quantitatively and qualitatively evaluated through comprehension tests and self-efficacy assessments with technical college students (aged 15-18), demonstrating their practical applicability and positive impact on student self-efficacy in actual educational environments. © 2025 IEEE.
