AD  - Technical University Berlin, Digital Engineering 4.0 Einstein Center Digital Future, Berlin, Germany
AD  - Industrial Information Technology, Technical University Berlin, Berlin, Germany
AB  - Engineering education is facing growing demands as technical systems become more complex, interconnected, and dynamic - creating new challenges for future engineers. To effectively prepare graduates for these challenges, engineering curricula must promote not only deep technical expertise but also cross-disciplinary and holistic thinking. In this context, Systems Thinking and Digital Engineering are emerging as critical competencies. However, these skills should not remain exclusive to specialized degree programs but instead be embedded as integral components of all engineering curricula. Drawing on the INCOSE Systems Engineering Competency Framework and the Stifterverband Future Skills Framework, we apply active learning strategies such as project-based learning, flipped classrooms, peer instruction, and gamification. Four modules are described in detail, illustrating how these frameworks and methods are addressed in practice. Through qualitative reflections on learning outcomes and student engagement, we highlight the benefits and challenges of this integrative approach. Finally, we discuss implications for curriculum development and the necessity of ongoing adaptation to evolving technological and educational contexts. © 2025 IEEE.
