AD  - Saudi Aramco, Saudi Arabia
AB  - Saudi Aramco's Drilling & Workover (D&WO) organizationsought to recruit competent Saudidrilling professionals to help implementits aggressive drilling operations plan. During the past few years, the D&WO organization was facing some difficulties attracting local professionals. In order to meet the national and global commitments, it became necessary to develop and retain additional Saudi drilling professionals to manage D&WO activities. This paper outlines how Saudi Aramco trains and develops local talent through its Rig Operations Program (ROP). This customizedprogram wasdeveloped internally by implementing mixed training methods. This strategyhelps to ensure the success of local talent entering the Saudi Aramco workforce. The Saudi Aramco D&WO Training Division has implemented this program to accelerate the development of recent Saudi engineering graduates. The goal is to prepare them to be world-class drilling engineers and drilling foremen. The ROP is unique in that it is developed and implemented by the Saudi Aramco D&WO Training Division in cooperation with drilling contractors. Multipletraining methods have been implemented to meet the objectives and requirements of each assignment throughout the three-year program duration. The ROP includes world-class classroom learning, e-Learning, knowledge transfer and competency based training. Mentorship and on-the-job training is also incorporated. In addition, the latest drilling engineering software, well control simulators and training rigs are integrated into this development program. The ROP has gone through several revisions and improvements since 2011. Now it has a solid structure that includesa rigorous evaluation system. This ensures high competency levels of the participants graduating from the training program. Through extensive program evaluation, we have determined that mentoring and employing diverse training methods have dramatically aided the development of theemployees. These methods have helped them to quickly contribute to their organizations by handling their regular dutiesin a shorter period of time. This paperoutlines the structure of the program, and how it is implemented by the D&WO Training Division. Alsoincluded are the evaluation findings and methods for tracking the participants' progress. In addition, measurement tools used to gauge the training program's success and major challenges are detailed. Lessons learned with recommendations stemming from the implementation and evaluation phases of the training program are discussed. Executing such a program is very costly in the short term. However, it provides a significant return on investment as talented Saudi professionals take over D&WO operations; thereby, reducing the need for expatriates. The ROP is an excellent example of how to quickly develop young drilling and workover foremen and engineers, and prepare them to professionally handle the complex tasks associated with D&WO operations. Copyright © 2017 Society of Petroleum Engineers.
