AD  - Universiti Malaysia Terengganu, Faculty of Science Computer and Mathematics, Terengganu, Kuala Nerus, Malaysia
AB  - As digital transformation continues to shape educational environments, learning analytics dashboard (LAD) are increasingly used to support outcome-based learning through data-driven insights. However, existing research has primarily focused on technical development with limited evaluation of LADs from the perspective of both lecturers and students in terms of information quality of it. Moreover, the ability of the current LADs to support meaningful interpretation of student competencies remains uncertain. This study presents the design and evaluation of a LAD incorporating descriptive, diagnostic, and predictive analytics aligned with Course Learning Outcomes (CLOs). The dashboard was tested among lecturers and students in Malaysian higher education settings. Using a mixed-methods approach, including structured surveys and usability scale, the study identifies which features from proposed LAD and existing LAD are more interpretable and actionable. Result reveal key differences in perception and usability preferences across user groups and suggest implications for improving dashboard design for effective academic interventions. © 2025 IEEE.
