AB  - Engineering education has long operated under a model that filters students out rather than welcomes them in. From high school onward, the field is often framed narrowly encouraging only students who excel in math and science, while overlooking students who show engineering promise through hands-on or creative problem-solving. Once enrolled, students frequently face gatekeeping courses, such as Calculus, which discourage persistence before they even take an engineering class. This outdated model disproportionately excludes women and students from underrepresented backgrounds, contributing to stagnant diversity in the field despite years of intervention. Instead of continuing to mold students to fit this rigid structure, engineering education must evolve to become more student-ready—meeting learners where they are and supporting a broader range of strengths and pathways into the profession. The 2024 ASEE Engineering Mindset Report [1], calls for a systemic transformation of engineering education to meet the demands of a changing world. In response, the University of Detroit Mercy is launching a pilot program to reimagine the first-year engineering experience. The initiative integrates math, science, and engineering in a cohesive, studio-style curriculum that introduces concepts as needed, rather than in rigid sequences. Traditional exams are replaced by competency-based assessments, allowing students to progress at their own pace. This paper explores the driving themes behind this transformation and outlines our approach to building a more inclusive, adaptable, and industry-aligned educational model. By redesigning the structure—not the student—we aim to open pathways for underrepresented groups and foster a more dynamic, resilient engineering workforce. Copyright © 2025 by ASME.
